/-
# The $940 hiding in a $50 bankroll

A $50 chip on red has a 47.4% chance of becoming $100 at American roulette.
Change it for singles and play until you double up or go broke. Now it's 0.51%.

Run locally with `haze bold_play.hz`. The two players put the same claim to the
checker at the end. One claim fails on purpose, so the file exits with status 1.
-/

/-
## Fifty chips on the rail

Put fifty singles on the rail and bet one on red. A win leaves 51. A loss on the
next spin leaves 50. The player is back where she started. In the dealer's book,
two dollars have already been bet.

Stop when the rail is empty or it holds a hundred chips. At $1 a spin, the spin
count is also the total wagered: about $940 on average, all from that first $50.
Red wins on 18 pockets and loses on 20, including 0 and 00. At even money the
house keeps 2/38 of each dollar wagered in expectation. Its take here is $49.49.
-/

system Flat(p : Num, stake : Num, start : Num) {
  var money : Num := start
  step {
    if money > 0 and money < 100 {
      money := money + (if flip(p) then stake else -stake)
    }
  }
}

#expect Flat(18/38, 1, 50) |= steps until money == 0 or money == 100  -- Spins to cash-out
#prob Flat(18/38, 1, 50) |= eventually money == 100                  -- Chance of $100

/-
## Leave the chip whole

Now hand the dealer one $50 chip. The house gets its edge on $50 once: an average
of $2.63. The chance of leaving with $100 is 9/19, about 92 times the chance
with singles.

At $60, bold play doesn't mean all in. It means bet $40: a win is enough, and a
loss leaves $20 to play with. Dubins and Savage proved this rule maximizes the
chance of reaching a target in an unfavorable even-money game without a table
limit (*How to Gamble If You Must*, 1965).

![Chance of reaching $100 by starting balance: at $50, bold play gives 47.4% and singles give 0.51%.](https://haze-lang.pages.dev/blog/images/bold-play-curve.png)
Figure 1. The fair game is the diagonal. Both solid lines use the American wheel.
-/

system Bold(p : Num, start : Num) {
  var money : Num := start
  step {
    if money > 0 and money < 100 {
      let stake := min(money, 100 - money)
      money := money + (if flip(p) then stake else -stake)
    }
  }
}

#prob Bold(18/38, 50) |= eventually money == 100      -- One $50 bet
#prob Flat(18/38, 5, 50) |= eventually money == 100   -- $5 per spin
#prob Flat(18/38, 10, 50) |= eventually money == 100  -- $10 per spin
#prob Flat(18/38, 25, 50) |= eventually money == 100  -- $25 per spin

/-
## Lift out the double zero

One change to the wheel: take out 00. Red still pays even money and a single
zero still takes the full stake. The one-chip player picks up a little over one
percentage point. For the $1 player, removing that one losing pocket makes
doubling more than twelve times as likely.

Either player could keep more money *on average* by walking away with the $50.
That wouldn't get them to $100. The same distinction between cash and a target
motivates [*Gambling for Goals*](https://www.nber.org/papers/w36010), the household
finance paper [discussed at Marginal Revolution](https://marginalrevolution.com/marginalrevolution/2026/11/gambling-for-goals.html).
-/

#prob Bold(18/37, 50) |= eventually money == 100     -- One $50 bet, single zero
#prob Flat(18/37, 1, 50) |= eventually money == 100  -- Singles, single zero

/-
## Back at sixty

Give the bold player $30. A red takes it to $60. A loss, two wins and another
loss take it through $20, $40 and $80, then back to $60. That loop can repeat
any number of times. `eventually` has no deadline: Haze solves one equation per
reachable balance rather than cutting the session off at the thousandth spin.

At $50 one spin settles the game. At $25 a win lands on $50; at $75 a loss does
the same. Write the balance as a fraction x of the goal: each spin that leaves
the game undecided takes it to 2x or 2x - 1. Either drops the first binary digit
after the point. Look for the copies of the $50 notch at quarters and eighths.
With arbitrarily fine stakes the success curve rises continuously but has slope
zero almost everywhere. [Billingsley gives the proof](https://www.jstor.org/stable/27852139).

![Shortfall from a fair wheel in percentage points, with notches at $25, $50 and $75.](https://haze-lang.pages.dev/blog/images/bold-play-shortfall.png)
Figure 2. Of 4,097 starting balances plotted, the largest shortfall is 3.57 points near $66.67.
-/

#prob Bold(18/38, 30) |= eventually money == 100                     -- Bold from $30
#prob Flat(18/38, 1, 30) |= eventually money == 100                  -- Singles from $30
#expect Bold(18/38, 30) |= steps until money == 0 or money == 100    -- Spins, bold from $30
#prob Bold(1/2, 30) |= eventually money == 100                       -- Bold on a fair wheel
#prob Flat(1/2, 1, 30) |= eventually money == 100                    -- Singles on a fair wheel

/-
## Rub out the odds, keep the ledger

Put the $50 back. This time the win rate is fixed but unknown. Your best estimate
is 49%; you give a favorable 50.5% much less weight. These are judgments, not
casino data. A possibility of 0.2 doesn't mean one session in five has good odds.
Only the spins are random.

Both players want at least a 40% chance of doubling. At 49% per spin, singles
get there about 12% of the time; at 50.5%, about 73%. One $50 bet just takes
the odds it's given: 49% to 50.5%. Haze checks the 40% requirement against
*belief*, which is 0.119175 for singles and 0.49 for one bet. The fifty straight
losses in the output illustrate a losing run; one streak can't refute a
probability bound. The combined probability of all the winning runs settles it.

Try swapping the weights 1 and 0.2 below. Even with 50.5% ranked first, the
$1 player still misses the target: belief rises to just 37.76%. The 49.5%
estimate still carries weight 0.7. [How Haze weighs the rows](https://haze-lang.pages.dev/docs/fuzzy/).
-/

param win : Num ~= {0.49: 1, 0.495: 0.7, 0.5: 0.4, 0.505: 0.2}

claim flat_doubles : Flat(win, 1, 50) |= eventually money == 100 with prob >= 0.4  -- $1 per spin
claim bold_doubles : Bold(win, 50) |= eventually money == 100 with prob >= 0.4     -- One $50 bet
